// std/iteration.dag -- Bounded iteration primitives (Layer 3-4).
//
// These are the ONLY iteration primitives in the .dag language. Every
// computation that repeats must go through one of these. There is no
// unbounded loop, no general recursion, no while(true). Decidability
// is a consequence of the primitives being closed over finite inputs.
//
// THE DECIDABILITY ARGUMENT:
//
//   1. Base values are finite (Bit has 2 inhabitants, Word64 has 2^64).
//   2. Every constructor preserves finiteness:
//      - Product/Coproduct: |A × B| = |A| · |B|, |A + B| = |A| + |B|
//      - Collection append: |list ++ [x]| = |list| + 1
//      - Node construction: children is a finite List
//   3. Every iteration primitive is bounded by a finite structure:
//      - fold:    bounded by |collection|
//      - descend: bounded by |tree|
//      - repeat:  bounded by N (explicit)
//   4. Composition of bounded operations is bounded.
//   5. No other iteration primitive exists.
//   6. Therefore: all .dag programs terminate.
//
// The developer doesn't think about decidability. They write programs
// using these primitives naturally. The compiler derives cost formulas
// from the primitive structure. Undecidable programs are not detected
// and rejected — they are structurally unrepresentable.
//
// WHAT ABOUT "INFINITE" PROGRAMS?
//
//   A server that handles requests "forever" uses:
//     repeat(bound: max_int, f: handle_request)
//
//   max_int is 2^63 - 1 = 9,223,372,036,854,775,807. At one request
//   per nanosecond, this runs for 292 years. At one per millisecond,
//   292 million years. The developer writes "serve forever." The
//   compiler sees "bounded iteration." Same program, different
//   semantics. The bound can be astronomically large.
//
// WHY NOT JUST CHECK?
//
//   A checker that detects unbounded recursion is a ratchet — it
//   catches violations after the fact. The primitives approach makes
//   violations unrepresentable. The difference:
//
//   - Checker: "your program has unbounded recursion" (error message)
//   - Primitives: unbounded recursion doesn't exist as a concept
//     (the language has no primitive for it)
//
//   This is the same principle as ExpectedToken making invalid parser
//   dispatch unrepresentable, or Connective making invalid structural
//   composition unrepresentable.
//
// RECURSIVE SYNTAX IS SUGAR:
//
//   Developers write recursive functions for familiarity:
//
//     fn simplify(expr: CostExpr) -> CostExpr {
//       match expr {
//         CostAdd { left: l, right: r } =>
//           CostAdd { left: simplify(l), right: simplify(r) }
//         _ => expr
//       }
//     }
//
//   The compiler recognizes this as a structural descent (each self-call
//   receives a child of the input) and lowers to:
//
//     descend(expr, f: (node, processed_children) => ...)
//
//   If the compiler cannot lower a recursive function to a bounded
//   primitive, compilation fails. This is not a heuristic — it is a
//   structural analysis of the call pattern:
//
//   | Call pattern                      | Lowers to           |
//   |-----------------------------------|---------------------|
//   | Self-call on child of input       | descend (tree fold) |
//   | Self-call inside fold body        | Already bounded     |
//   | Self-call with n - 1              | repeat(n, ...)      |
//   | Self-call with same argument      | REJECTED            |
//
//   "REJECTED" means the function cannot be expressed as bounded
//   iteration. This is a compiler error, not a user error — the
//   language does not have unbounded recursion as a concept.
//
// RELATIONSHIP TO COMPOSITION STACK:
//
//   Layer 3 (Collection algebras): fold, map, filter, flat_map
//     - Bounded by |collection|
//     - Already exist as methods on List, Set, Map
//
//   Layer 4 (Structural compositions): descend, repeat
//     - descend: bounded by |tree| (structural recursion)
//     - repeat: bounded by N (explicit count)
//     - This file fills the "MISSING FROM STD" gap
//
// CLOSURE PROPERTY:
//
//   If someone builds a DSL on top of .dag, that DSL is also decidable.
//   The DSL is composed from .dag primitives, which are all bounded.
//   There is no escape hatch — you cannot construct an unbounded
//   computation from bounded parts.

module std.iteration

// =========================================================================
// descend: structural recursion over tree-shaped data
//
// Process each node of a tree bottom-up. Children are processed first;
// the combining function receives the node and its children's results.
//
// Bounded by |tree| (total number of nodes). Each node is visited
// exactly once. The combining function runs once per node.
//
// This is a catamorphism — the fundamental elimination form for
// inductively-defined tree structures, just as fold is the fundamental
// elimination form for lists.
//
// Cost: O(|tree| × per_node_cost)
// =========================================================================

// descend is a primitive operation on tree-shaped structures.
// The compiler provides the implementation; this declaration
// establishes the concept in the type algebra.
//
// For Node (the universal carrier):
//   descend(node, f) = f(node, node.children |> map(child => descend(child, f)))
//
// For any inductively-defined type with children:
//   descend(tree, children_of, f) = f(tree, children_of(tree) |> map(c => descend(c, children_of, f)))

// =========================================================================
// repeat: bounded counted iteration
//
// Execute a step function exactly N times, threading an accumulator.
// N can be any non-negative integer, including astronomically large
// values (2^63 - 1 = ~9.2 quintillion).
//
// This is the primitive for "do something N times" where N is known
// or bounded at compile time. It replaces while loops, general loops,
// and counted for-loops.
//
// Cost: O(N × per_step_cost)
// =========================================================================

// repeat is sugar for fold over an implicit range [0, N).
// repeat(bound: N, init: x, f: step) ≡ fold(range(0, N), init: x, f: (acc, _) => step(acc))

// =========================================================================
// repeat_until: bounded iteration with early exit
//
// Like repeat, but stops early when a condition is met. Still bounded
// by N — the condition is an optimization, not a correctness concern.
// The program terminates whether or not the condition is ever true.
//
// Cost: O(min(N, k) × per_step_cost) where k = first iteration where done(acc) is true
// =========================================================================

// repeat_until(bound: N, init: x, f: step, done: check)
// ≡ fold(range(0, N), init: x, f: (acc, _) => if done(acc) { acc } else { step(acc) })
